<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siglo XX mine</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Siglo XX is a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> mine at Llallagua in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, developed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-patino">Simón Patiño</a> in the early twentieth century and nationalised in 1952. With the neighbouring Catavi complex it became the heartland of Bolivia's militant miners' unions. Workers there were killed in the Catavi massacre of 1942 and the San Juan massacre of 1967.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siglo-xx-mine.jpg" alt="Siglo XX mine" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Office of War Information, Overseas Picture Division, Washington Division · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siglo_XX.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From about 1900 tin replaced silver as the main export, making the mine owners Simón Patiño, Mauricio Hochschild and the Aramayo family the most powerful men in the country and turning mining camps such as Catavi and <b>Siglo XX</b> into centres of militant unionism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-bolivia">Republic of Bolivia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
